---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy policy — Depesha"
  description="Privacy policy of the Depesha website and desktop app: no cookies, no analytics, no trackers; your mail stays on your computer and your mail server."
  lang="en"
  alternates={{ ru: '/privacy/', en: '/en/privacy/' }}
>
  <main class="legal">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1 class="sec-title">Privacy <em>policy</em></h1>
      <p class="legal-date">Effective date: 18 October 2026.</p>
      <div class="legal-body">
        <p>
          This is the privacy policy of the website depesha-app.ru and of the desktop application
          Depesha (Депеша), called the app below. In short: the website collects nothing about you,
          and the app works with your mail directly, without passing it on to anyone.
        </p>

        <h2>What data the app accesses</h2>
        <p>
          Depesha receives mail and sends mail. To do that it connects to the mail servers you already
          have and works with the data they hold, and with what you keep on your own computer. The
          app contains no telemetry and sends the developer no usage statistics, no message contents
          and no information about your accounts.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>Mail.</b> Messages are received and sent directly between the app and your mail server
            over IMAP, SMTP or Exchange Web Services: message headers and bodies, attachments,
            folders, flags and labels. The app has no server of its own, and your mail does not pass
            through one.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Passwords and tokens.</b> Mailbox passwords and OAuth refresh tokens are kept in the
            operating system keychain (Secret Service on Linux, Keychain on macOS, Credential Manager
            on Windows) and are never written to files or logs. The access token for mail lives in
            memory only and never reaches the disk.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Sign-in with Google, Yandex and Microsoft.</b> Sign-in happens in the system browser
            over OAuth 2.0 with PKCE; the answer comes back to a one-shot address 127.0.0.1 on your
            own computer. The app then logs in to the mail server with the issued token (XOAUTH2).
            The account's address and name are used to label the mailbox in the app.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Update checks.</b> The app occasionally asks GitHub whether a new version exists. You
            can switch the check off in Settings. Every update is signed and verified with a key
            built into the app.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Sender logos.</b> Next to a message the app may show a company logo when the message
            passed the sender's authentication (DMARC). For that it asks the sender's domain for the
            DNS records <code>_dmarc.domain</code> and <code>default._bimi.domain</code> and, if the
            domain published such a logo, downloads it over HTTPS from that same domain. A forged
            message gets no logo. A logo is requested at most once a week per domain, and logos can
            be switched off in the mail settings.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Server settings.</b> When you add a mailbox the app picks the incoming and outgoing
            servers for you. It asks the domain's DNS for MX and SRV records, reads configuration
            files at <code>autoconfig.domain</code>, <code>domain/.well-known/autoconfig</code> and
            <code>autoconfig.thunderbird.net</code>, and probes ordinary addresses such as
            <code>mail.domain</code> and <code>imap.domain</code>. For Exchange it looks up the
            Autodiscover address the same way. All of this happens while the mailbox is being set up.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Unsubscribing.</b> If you press “Unsubscribe”, the app may send a request to the
            newsletter's server (one-click unsubscribe) or a message from your mailbox — only after
            you confirm it, and only to the address the sender gave.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Extensions.</b> Third-party extensions that you installed yourself go out only to the
            servers named in their description and confirmed by you at installation.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Images and links in messages.</b> Remote images and read receipts are not loaded by
            default, and before opening a link the app shows you where it really leads.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Local data.</b> Settings, the mail cache, the search index and your drafts are stored
            in the app's data folder on your computer.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How it is used</h2>
        <p>
          Only to do what you ask of a mail client: receive, show, search and sort your mail, sort it
          into folders, snooze it, reply, forward and send, and keep it readable offline. There is no
          other purpose. The data is not used for advertising, profiling or analytics, is not used to
          train artificial intelligence models, and is not sold, rented or transferred to anyone.
        </p>

        <h2>Storage and security</h2>
        <p>
          Everything the app keeps stays on your computer, and the copies of your messages stay on
          your mail server. There is no Depesha server, no cloud account and no backup held by the
          developer. Passwords and refresh tokens are stored in the operating system keychain; the
          access token exists only in the app's memory while it runs. Mail is transferred over
          encrypted connections (TLS) to the servers you configured, and the app refuses to send a
          password unencrypted unless you turn that on yourself, with a warning. The source code is
          open (MIT OR Apache-2.0), so all of this can be checked.
        </p>

        <h2>Sharing</h2>
        <p>
          Depesha does not sell, rent, trade or otherwise transfer your data to third parties. Your
          mail, attachments, contacts and account details travel only between your computer and your
          mail servers. The app has no advertising, no analytics service, no crash-reporting service
          and no tracking of any kind. Nobody reads your mail: the developer has no access to it, and
          there is no human review of user data. The one exception is what you do yourself — the
          service requests listed above (a sender's DNS records and logo, a newsletter's unsubscribe
          address, or servers named by an extension you installed), each of which goes only to that
          server.
        </p>

        <h2>Retention and deletion</h2>
        <p>
          Data is kept for as long as the mailbox is set up in the app. To delete it, remove the
          account in the app's settings: the app erases that account's cached messages, message
          bodies, search index and service records, and removes its password or token from the
          operating system keychain. Deleting the app deletes its data folder with it. Messages on
          the server are not deleted by any of this: they belong to your mail provider, and you
          delete them there, in the app or in webmail.
        </p>

        <h2>Google user data</h2>
        <p>
          Signing in with Google is one of the ways to add a mailbox; the app also works without it,
          with any IMAP/SMTP server. If you sign in with Google, the app asks Google for:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b><code>https://mail.google.com/</code> — mail.</b> The app connects to Gmail over IMAP
            and SMTP with the issued token to read, show, organize and send your own mail: apply
            labels, archive, move and delete messages, send your replies and drafts. The app needs no
            other access to the mailbox.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b><code>openid</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>profile</code> — the account.</b> The
            address and the name, to label the mailbox in the list and to show which address you
            signed in with.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <b>How Google user data is used.</b> Only to receive, show, organize and send your mail on
          your computer. Google user data is not used for advertising, profiling, analytics, training
          of artificial intelligence models or any other purpose.
        </p>
        <p>
          <b>Where it is stored.</b> The refresh token is kept in the operating system keychain, the
          access token in memory, and mail and its cache in the app's data folder on your disk. The
          app has no server of its own, so Google user data does not leave your computer except in
          the exchange with Google's servers.
        </p>
        <p>
          <b>Sharing.</b> The app does not sell, transfer or disclose Google user data to anyone. The
          data goes only between your computer and Google's servers (OAuth, IMAP, SMTP). The
          developer and staff have no access to your mail and do not read it: there is no human
          reading of the data.
        </p>
        <p>
          <b>Retention and deletion of Google user data.</b> It is kept for as long as the mailbox is
          set up in the app. To delete it, remove the account in the app's settings: the mail cache
          and the token are deleted. You can revoke the app's access to your Google account at any
          time at
          <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions">myaccount.google.com/permissions</a>;
          after that the issued token stops working. Deleting the app also deletes its data folder.
        </p>
        <p>
          Depesha's use and transfer to any other app of information received from Google APIs will
          adhere to the
          <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
            >Google API Services User Data Policy</a
          >, including the Limited Use requirements.
        </p>

        <h2>The website</h2>
        <p>
          The website is a set of static pages. It sets no cookies and uses no analytics, visitor
          counters, advertising or trackers. Fonts, the logo and all images are served from our own
          domain. The only external server the page talks to is api.github.com: once when it opens
          (and not more than once in 10 minutes) it asks GitHub for the latest version number, so
          that the buttons and download links point at current files. The request carries no cookie
          and no page address (no-referrer); GitHub sees your IP address, as it does for any request
          to its server. If no answer arrives, the page shows the version recorded at build time.
        </p>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          The source code of the app is open, and everything above can be checked in it. Questions
          and remarks go to the
          <a href="https://github.com/fgbm/depesha/issues">repository issues</a>. Please report
          vulnerabilities privately through the
          <a href="https://github.com/fgbm/depesha/security/policy">security policy</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes</h2>
        <p>
          When this policy changes, a new revision with a new effective date appears on this page.
          A new revision applies to new versions of the app and does not widen access to data the app
          has already received.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .legal {
    padding: 150px 0 110px;
  }
  .legal-date {
    margin-top: 16px;
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .legal-body {
    margin-top: 40px;
    max-width: 46em;
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .legal-body h2 {
    margin-top: 20px;
    font: 600 clamp(24px, 2.4vw, 32px) / 1.15 var(--serif);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .legal-body ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: 14px;
    padding-left: 1.2em;
  }
  .legal-body li {
    padding-left: 4px;
  }
  .legal-body a {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .legal-body code {
    font: 500 0.92em / 1 var(--sans);
    background: var(--paper-2);
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  /* Длинные адреса и токены в коде переносятся, иначе узкий экран получает
     горизонтальную прокрутку (проверяет scripts/check-overflow.mjs). */
  .legal-body code,
  .legal-body a {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
